# serial_cycle_shadow -- a standby written BESIDE the domain it would
# replace, and the reason a ring refuses to read it at all.
#
# The tempting repair for a lost middle is to name its replacement in
# advance: let `beech` hold exactly what `birch` holds, so the day birch is
# declined the ring already has a hand on its regions. This declaration
# writes that idea out plainly and it stops one layer earlier than a
# capability check.
#
# `ask_ab` now has two readers, birch and beech. `ask_bc` now has two
# writers, birch and beech. The flow derivation asks each region for
# exactly one writer and exactly one reader, and this document answers two
# of each -- so there is no directed flow to derive, no shape to name, and
# nothing for a ring order to search.
#
# The lesson is about time rather than rights. A standby that holds the
# lost domain's regions while that domain is still present is not a spare;
# it is a second writer, and a second writer is what a directed region
# exists to rule out. The cost of a detour drawn in advance is charged
# every day the hole is closed.
#
# Read whole by `caravan/read.rye`; refused by `caravan/standby.rye`.

system serial_cycle_shadow

domain alder
domain birch
domain beech
domain cedar

channel alder birch
channel birch cedar
channel cedar alder
channel alder beech
channel beech cedar

region ask_ab 4096
region ask_bc 4096
region ask_ca 4096
region taken 64

grant ask_ab alder rw
grant ask_ab birch r
grant ask_ab beech r
grant ask_bc birch rw
grant ask_bc beech rw
grant ask_bc cedar r
grant ask_ca cedar rw
grant ask_ca alder r
grant taken alder rw
grant taken cedar r
